From fd648dcb3fedf7d0b64cb97302bd1bfcc91742b3 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Filyus <filyus@filyus.ru>
Date: Thu, 15 Oct 2026 02:35:57 +0500
Subject: [PATCH] feat(config): accept tokenHelper from user and global config
 only

Recognizes `//<registry>/:tokenHelper` as a nerf-dart key, so a ~/.npmrc
shared with pnpm no longer warns about it as unknown config. The key names
a program npm executes, so it is honored only from user and global config,
and only when the setting that selected that file (`userconfig`,
`globalconfig`, or the `prefix` the default global file derives from) did
not come from a project .npmrc: project config is loaded first and could
otherwise point npm at a repository file. The origin is read from what each
layer was given, with keys compared after environment expansion (so
`user${UNSET?}config=` counts), because a project `globalconfig` is stored
on the default layer through its accessor. Files the parent did not trust
are passed to child npm processes in `npm_config_token_helper_untrusted`,
since a child inherits the chosen paths as environment values. A
tokenHelper from project config, publishConfig, the environment or the
command line is ignored with a warning; `npm config set` refuses the
unscoped form and project config, and `npm config ls` marks a publishConfig
tokenHelper as ignored.

The unscoped pnpm form for the default registry is not supported, since a
project can change which registry is the default; it gets a warning that
points to the scoped form and is not fatal under strict-npmrc.

Commands that check for a login before sending a request (publish, whoami
and anything using get-identity) count a tokenHelper as a credential;
logout explains that the helper setting has to be removed and names a token
stored beside it; profile enable-2fa says it cannot work through a helper;
an OIDC trusted-publishing token masks a configured helper while in use. An
error cause without a message no longer prints "cause undefined".

Pairs with the npm-registry-fetch prototype that runs the helper. Prototype
for the credential provider RFC discussed in npm/rfcs#850.
