diff --git a/docs/lib/content/configuring-npm/npmrc.md b/docs/lib/content/configuring-npm/npmrc.md
index b1e7d16..db39bed 100644
--- a/docs/lib/content/configuring-npm/npmrc.md
+++ b/docs/lib/content/configuring-npm/npmrc.md
@@ -87,7 +87,7 @@ This is primarily for distribution maintainers to override default configs in a
 
 ### Auth related configuration
 
-The settings `_auth`, `_authToken`, `username`, `_password`, `certfile`, and `keyfile` must all be scoped to a specific registry.
+The settings `_auth`, `_authToken`, `username`, `_password`, `certfile`, `keyfile`, and `tokenHelper` must all be scoped to a specific registry.
 This ensures that `npm` will never send credentials to the wrong host.
 
 The full list is:
@@ -99,12 +99,25 @@ The full list is:
  - `cafile` (path to certificate authority file)
  - `certfile` (path to certificate file)
  - `keyfile` (path to key file)
+ - `tokenHelper` (absolute path to a program that prints a token, see below)
 
 In order to scope these values, they must be prefixed by a URI fragment.
 If the credential is meant for any request to a registry on a single host, the scope may look like `//registry.npmjs.org/:`.
 If it must be scoped to a specific path on the host that path may also be provided, such as
 `//my-custom-registry.org/unique/path:`.
 
+#### Token helpers
+
+`tokenHelper` names a program npm runs to obtain the credential for a registry, instead of reading a token stored in the file, compatible with pnpm's setting of the same name:
+
+```ini
+//npm.corp.example.com/:tokenHelper=/usr/local/bin/corp-registry-token
+```
+
+The value is an absolute path with no arguments; it may contain spaces, and unlike pnpm, npm does not split it into a command and arguments. npm runs the program the first time a request to that registry needs a credential, at most once per npm process, with an empty stdin and a 60 second timeout. Its stdout is the credential: a raw token is sent as `Authorization: Bearer <token>`, and output that already starts with an auth scheme, such as `Basic …`, is used as the header value. Lines the program writes to stderr are shown as warnings. If the program fails, prints nothing, prints more than one line or characters outside printable ASCII, or times out, the request fails; npm does not fall back to another credential for that registry. When a registry has both a `tokenHelper` and a stored credential, the helper is used, so `npm login` refuses for that registry and `npm logout` explains that the setting has to be removed.
+
+Because npm executes the program, `tokenHelper` is honored only from the per-user and global config files, and only when the setting that selected the file (`userconfig`, `globalconfig`, `prefix`) did not come from a project `.npmrc`, and only when the file does not lie inside a project, meaning any directory from the current one up that has a `package.json` or `node_modules`, or a `--prefix` outside a global run: `npm --prefix client` makes `client/etc/npmrc` the global config file, and a file in a checkout is the repository's whoever points npm at it. The home directory does not count as a project, and the default `~/.npmrc` and global config file stay trusted. The program starts in its own directory and does not receive `NODE_OPTIONS`, `npm_config_*` or the other variables npm sets from its config. It is ignored with a warning in a project `.npmrc`, in `publishConfig`, in the environment and on the command line. Unlike pnpm, npm does not accept an unscoped `tokenHelper` for the default registry, since a project can change which registry is the default, nor a helper per package scope (`//host/:@scope:tokenHelper`); both are ignored with a warning.
+
 ### Unsupported Custom Configuration Keys
 
 Starting in npm v11.2.0, npm warns when unknown configuration keys are defined in `.npmrc`. In a future major version of npm, these unknown keys may no longer be accepted.
